<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Felix Houphouet-Boigny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Félix Houphouët-Boigny was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, from independence in 1960 until his death in 1993. A physician and wealthy planter, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-agricultural-union">African Agricultural Union</a> in 1944, sat in the French National Assembly, where he secured the abolition of forced labour in 1946, and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rassemblement-democratique-africain">African Democratic Rally</a>. He kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and presided over a long export-led boom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny.jpg" alt="Félix Houphouët-Boigny" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GPO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%A9lix_Houphou%C3%ABt-Boigny_1962-07-16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
